  "body": "When a dataset has a wild value that does not fit, a box plot uses fences to spot it. We look at the middle spread of the numbers, called the interquartile range, to draw these secret lines. Look at the box plot to see where the fences sit compared to the extreme point.\\n\\n[[visual:1]]\\n\\nFor the sorted data 3, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 11, 12, 40, the first quartile is 6 and the third quartile is 9. The interquartile range is the gap between them, which is 3.\\n\\nFences are placed $1.5$ times this range beyond the quartiles. This creates an upper fence at $9 + 4.5 = 13.5$ and a lower fence at $6 - 4.5 = 1.5$. Any data point lying past these fences, such as 40, is flagged as an outlier.",
  "questions": [
    "What happens if the data has multiple outliers?",
    "Why do we use 1.5 times the IQR instead of another multiplier?",
    "How does a skewed dataset affect the median and quartiles?"
  ],
  "terms": [
    {
      "t": "interquartile range",
      "d": "The measure of statistical dispersion, being the difference between the upper and lower quartiles.",
      "n": 0
    },
    {
      "t": "outlier",
      "d": "An observation that lies an abnormal distance from other values in a random sample.",
      "n": 0
    }
  ]
}

